1 0 0 1 54 613 Tm
(What problem does Guardian Shell solve?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 590 Tm
(It governs what a specific AI agent session can access on a Linux machine without) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 575 Tm
(forcing the whole developer workflow into a heavy container or VM model.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 550 Tm
(Why is that different from normal Linux permissions?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 527 Tm
(Because Linux file permissions usually control what a user can access. Guardian Shell is) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 512 Tm
(designed to control what one agent session can access, even if that agent is running) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 497 Tm
(under the same user account as the developer.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 472 Tm
(Why use eBPF for LLM agents?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 449 Tm
(Because eBPF lets the product observe and enforce policy at the kernel boundary, per) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 434 Tm
(agent session, in real time. That is what enables:) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 409 Tm
(- session-aware policy) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 388 Tm
(- subprocess tracking) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 367 Tm
(- live audit trails) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 346 Tm
(- temporary approvals) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 325 Tm
(- dashboard visibility) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 304 Tm
(Why not just use chmod, ACLs, AppArmor, or SELinux?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 281 Tm
(Because those tools solve adjacent problems, not the entire product problem.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 256 Tm
(- chmod and ACLs are useful baseline controls, but they are not session-aware) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 235 Tm
